const sections=[{"id":"section-1","heading":"Is there an English translation of Martín Fierro?","paragraphs":["Walter Owen's English rendering, published in 1935 under the title The Gaucho Martín Fierro, is a documented historical translation. A digitized facsimile of the edition can be consulted through Wikimedia Commons. Its availability online does not automatically establish that every jurisdiction permits unrestricted republication.","This website does not reproduce Owen's complete English text in its own reader. The original poem in Spanish is available here with canto-by-canto navigation."]},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Why translating gaucho poetry is difficult","paragraphs":["The narrator speaks through a carefully constructed literary version of rural speech. Words such as gaucho, payada, pulpería and fortín carry histories that a brief English equivalent may obscure. Translating a payada is not simply transferring its dictionary meanings: rhythm, rhyme, voice and performance also matter.","English renderings may choose to preserve Spanish terms, add explanatory notes, imitate a rhyme pattern or prioritize clarity. None of these decisions is neutral. A reader benefits from comparing a translation with the original when possible."]},{"id":"section-3","heading":"The original poem has two publication dates","paragraphs":["El gaucho Martín Fierro first appeared in 1872. La vuelta de Martín Fierro, a continuation rather than a later revision of the same text, followed in 1879. The two volumes differ in structure and emphasis: the first tells of loss, conscription and flight; the second includes recollection, other characters' testimony and advice.","A modern website that calls the entire story simply 'the 1872 poem' misses the importance of the 1879 continuation. Bibliographic records should identify which part and edition they describe."]},{"id":"section-4","heading":"How to find reliable editions","paragraphs":["A scanned historical volume is a facsimile; a typed digital text is a transcription; a modern English summary is neither. Even when sources present similar words, their provenance and editorial status differ. The Biblioteca Virtual Miguel de Cervantes and our archive provide useful reference points for identifying editions.","For a first approach, read an English overview, consult the digitized Owen volume, and open the corresponding canto in Spanish. This method provides context without passing off an original English explanatory article as a translation by Hernández."]},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Can you quote the translation freely?","paragraphs":["The public-domain status of Hernández's nineteenth-century Spanish writings should not be confused with the rights attached to a twentieth-century English translation. Laws and expiry periods vary across countries, and a hosted PDF is not by itself a licence for unrestricted republication.","We link to archival records and editions instead of copying the translation wholesale. Readers looking for a recent commercial translation should check the publisher, translator, date and rights information for that specific edition."]}];
const sources=[{"name":"Walter Owen's historical English translation (Wikimedia Commons facsimile)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Gaucho_Martin_Fierro_-_Jose_Hernandez.pdf"},{"name":"Biblioteca Virtual Miguel de Cervantes — José Hernández","url":"https://www.cervantesvirtual.com/portales/jose_hernandez/"},{"name":"English introduction to Martín Fierro","url":"/en/what-is-martin-fierro/"},{"name":"MARTINFIERRO.ORG documentary archive","url":"/en/archive/"}];
